{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>This is the sign of the covenant which I am giving.</b> The implication of this sign is that there was no rainbow in the cloud from the works of Creation, and now HaShem created something new to make a rainbow in the sky on a cloudy day. And they said concerning the reason for this sign, that He did not make the bow with its legs upward, so that it would appear as though from the heavens they are aiming at it — \"and He sent out His arrows and scattered them\" (Psalms 18:15) — but rather He made it in the opposite manner, to show that they do not aim at it from the heavens. And so is the way of warriors, to turn it upside down in their hand when they call for peace to those opposite them. Furthermore, a bow has no string with which to set arrows upon it. And we perforce must believe the words of the Greeks, that from the heat of the sun in moist air the rainbow comes into being, for with a vessel of water before the sun the appearance of a rainbow can be seen. And when we further examine the language of the verse we understand accordingly, for He said, \"My bow I have set in the cloud\" and did not say \"I am setting in the cloud\" as He said, \"This is the sign of the covenant which I am giving.\" And the phrase \"My bow\" indicates that the bow had existed beforehand. Therefore we interpret the verse thus: the bow which I have set in the cloud since the day of Creation shall be, from this day forward, a sign of covenant between Me and you — that whenever I see it I will recall that a covenant of peace exists between Me and you. And if you ask what reason there is for the bow to serve as a sign, it is like the reason of \"this heap is a witness, and the pillar is a witness\" (Genesis 31:52), and likewise \"these seven ewes you shall take from my hand, that they may be a witness to me\" (Genesis 21:30), for any visible object placed before two parties to remind them of a matter vowed between them is called a sign, and any agreement is a covenant. And so regarding circumcision He said, \"and it shall be a sign of the covenant between Me and you\" (Genesis 17:11), because of the agreement that all the seed of Abraham shall circumcise themselves to serve Him as one. Furthermore, when it is seen inverted, as mentioned, it serves as a remembrance of peace, as we have written. And whether the bow came into being now or whether it had always existed in nature, the reason of the sign in it is one and the same. But our Rabbis have in this passage a hidden secret. They said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 35:3): \"My bow I have set in the cloud\" — קַשְׁתִּי, my bow, a thing that is hard for Me. Is it possible? Rather, קָשִׁין דְּפִרְיָא, knots of fruit. \"And it shall be, when I cloud a cloud over the earth\" — Rabbi Yudan said in the name of Rabbi Yehuda ben Rabbi Simon: a parable of one who has boiling dish in his hand and wishes to put it on his son, but puts it on his servant instead. And there they further said: \"And the bow shall be in the cloud, and I will see it, to remember the everlasting covenant between Elohim\" — this is the attribute of judgment above; \"and between every living creature of all flesh that is upon the earth\" — this is the attribute of judgment below. The attribute of judgment above is severe, and the attribute of judgment below is weak. And you already know their statement (Hagigah 16a) concerning one who gazes at the rainbow: whoever has no pity for the honor of his Maker deserves not to have come into the world. And if you merit to understand their words, you will know that the interpretation of the verse is thus: My bow — which is the attribute of judgment, placed in the cloud at the time of judgment — shall be a sign of the covenant." "<b>And it shall be, when I cloud a cloud over the earth.</b> That HaShem will not illuminate His face toward it because of the sins of its inhabitants, and the attribute of judgment of Mine will be seen in the cloud, and then I will remember the covenant through the remembrance of mercy, and I will have compassion upon the young ones who are upon the earth. And this sign and this covenant is the sign of circumcision and the covenant that is in it, and the language of the biblical verses fits the matter very well. And here they interpreted \"between Elohim\" — the attribute of judgment above, which is Gevurah — and \"that is upon the earth\" — the attribute of judgment below, which is a gentle attribute governing the earth together with mercy, for He did not say \"which is in the earth\" but only \"which is upon the earth,\" and I have already hinted (Ramban on Genesis 6:13) at their secret in the name of the Earth. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 9:16): \"between the attribute of judgment above and you.\" But our Rabbis did not intend this."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The parable of סֹלֶת רוֹתֵחַ in Bereshit Rabbah is rendered literally as 'boiling dish'; the term can denote hot porridge or some other heated foodstuff, and no further specification is warranted by the context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:27:31.691172343Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}